/*
 * Linker version script for the shared library (ELF only).
 *
 * Hidden visibility gets us most of the way, but not all of it: libstdc++
 * declares `namespace std _GLIBCXX_VISIBILITY(default)`, which overrides
 * -fvisibility=hidden for everything in std::. Template members defined
 * out-of-line in the .tcc headers — std::_Hashtable::_M_insert_unique_node,
 * std::_Rb_tree::_M_get_insert_unique_pos and friends — are not implicitly
 * inline either, so -fvisibility-inlines-hidden does not reach them. They are
 * emitted into our objects as weak symbols with default visibility, and
 * --exclude-libs,ALL cannot help because they arrive from our own compilation
 * rather than from a static archive.
 *
 * Measured on the first CI run of the shared-build check: 95 exported symbols,
 * of which 1 was ours and 94 were std:: instantiations of exactly that kind.
 * This script drops them, so the dynamic table is the public API and nothing
 * else.
 *
 * This is not merely cosmetic. Exported vague-linkage instantiations
 * participate in symbol interposition, which is the same class of hazard §10
 * invokes for the Python extension — two extensions in one interpreter, built
 * against different standard libraries, resolving each other's std:: internals.
 *
 * Anonymous (no version node name) on purpose: a named node would decorate
 * every symbol with @@NODEHAMMER and add an ABS symbol for the node itself,
 * neither of which we want when the goal is an export table that reads exactly
 * like the API.
 *
 * Note what it deliberately does NOT do: `nodehammer::*` matches nested
 * namespaces too, so an internal symbol wrongly marked NH_API still gets
 * exported. That is ci/check_shared_exports.py's job, and the division is
 * intentional — the linker removes what is not ours, the checker removes what
 * is ours but should not be public. Verified against both.
 *
 * Comments here are C-style because GNU ld's version-script grammar has no
 * line comments: a `//` is parsed as two stray `/` characters and the script
 * fails with "syntax error in VERSION script".
 */
{
  global:
    extern "C++" {
      nodehammer::*;
    };
  local:
    *;
};
